A beloved weeknight family staple across the American Midwest. Known affectionately as American goulash or slumgullion, this one-pot dish simmers raw elbow macaroni directly in a rich, savory beef broth and tomato sauce with seasoned ground beef, aromatic herbs, sweet paprika, and gooey melted cheddar.

*Adapted from [Delish](https://www.delish.com/cooking/recipe-ideas/recipes/a55501/best-goulash-recipe/).*

## Ingredients

- 2 tablespoons extra-virgin olive oil
- 1 medium yellow onion, finely diced
- 2 cloves garlic, minced
- 1 lb lean ground beef (85% or 90% lean)
- 1 teaspoon kosher salt (plus more to taste)
- ½ teaspoon freshly cracked black pepper
- 1 tablespoon tomato paste
- 1 ¼ cups low-sodium beef broth
- 1 can (15 oz) plain tomato sauce
- 1 can (15 oz) petite diced tomatoes (undrained)
- 1 teaspoon dried Italian seasoning (oregano, basil, thyme)
- 1 teaspoon sweet Hungarian paprika
- 1 ½ cups uncooked elbow macaroni
- 1 cup shredded sharp cheddar cheese
- 2 tablespoons fresh flat-leaf parsley, finely chopped (for garnish)

## Instructions

1. **Sauté the Onion and Garlic:** In a large 12-inch deep skillet, sauté pan, or Dutch oven, heat 2 tablespoons of olive oil over medium heat. Add the diced yellow onion and cook, stirring occasionally, for 4 to 5 minutes until translucent and tender. Stir in the minced garlic and cook for 60 seconds until fragrant.
2. **Brown and Season Ground Beef:** Push the onions aside and add the ground beef to the hot pan. Break up the meat with a wooden spoon or spatula into small crumbles. Cook for 5 to 6 minutes until the beef is fully browned and no pink remains. Tilt the pan and spoon out excess grease (or blot with paper towels), then season the meat with kosher salt and black pepper.
3. **Incorporate Tomato Paste and Liquids:** Stir in the tomato paste, cooking for 1 minute to deepen its color and richness. Pour in the beef broth, tomato sauce, and canned diced tomatoes with all their liquid. Stir in the Italian seasoning and sweet paprika.
4. **Simmer the Uncooked Pasta:** Pour the dry, uncooked elbow macaroni directly into the simmering sauce. Stir well to submerge the pasta into the liquid. Bring the liquid to a lively bubble, then reduce heat to medium-low to maintain a gentle simmer.
5. **Cook to Al Dente:** Cook uncovered for 12 to 15 minutes, stirring frequently so the pasta starches don't settle or stick to the bottom of the pan. As the pasta absorbs the savory broth, the sauce will thicken into a rich, glossy ragu. Check pasta for doneness; it should be tender with a slight chew.
6. **Melt the Cheese and Garnish:** Take the skillet off the heat. Sprinkle the shredded sharp cheddar cheese evenly across the top. Stir gently until the cheese completely melts into the sauce, giving the goulash a creamy, luxurious finish. Taste and adjust salt and pepper. Sprinkle with fresh parsley and ladle into shallow bowls.

## Notes

- **Starch Secret:** Cooking the macaroni directly in the sauce allows the pasta starches to thicken the broth naturally into an unctuous, rich consistency without needing cornstarch or flour.
- **Vegetable Add-ins:** Feel free to dice a green or red bell pepper and sauté it along with the onion, or toss in a cup of frozen sweet corn or peas during the last 5 minutes of pasta simmering.
- **Storage and Reheating:** Keeps refrigerated in an airtight container for up to 4 days. As it sits, the pasta will continue absorbing sauce; when reheating, stir in a splash of water or beef broth to restore its saucy texture.
